    {
     "type": "tip",
     "tag": "Language focus",
     "title": "Do not or Don't?",
     "steps": [
      [
       "Announcement",
       "Do not use the lift."
      ],
      [
       "Person to person",
       "Don't run on the stairs."
      ],
      [
       "Report",
       "Kumar isn't here."
      ]
     ],
     "paras": [
      "Announcements and signs use the full form: <b>Do not</b>. It's slow and clear.",
      "People talking use the short form: <b>don't</b>, <b>isn't</b>, <b>aren't</b>. The meaning is the same."
     ],
     "avoid": "Not use the lift. · No run. · Kumar no is here."
    },
    {
     "type": "choice",
     "q": "Tanya to Diego: \"___ run on the stairs!\"",
     "opts": [
      "Don't",
      "No",
      "Not"
     ],
     "a": 0
    },
    {
     "type": "choice",
     "q": "A sign on the lift door says…",
     "opts": [
      "DO NOT USE IN AN EMERGENCY",
      "NO USE IN EMERGENCY",
      "NOT USE IN EMERGENCY"
     ],
     "a": 0
    },
    {
     "type": "build",
     "situation": "Kumar is not at the muster station.",
     "q": "Tell the Chief Officer.",
     "a": [
      "Kumar",
      "isn't",
      "here."
     ],
     "extra": [
      "no",
      "don't"
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "tip",
     "tag": "Spanish speaker alert",
     "title": "\"No\" is not \"not\"",
     "paras": [
      "In Spanish, <i>no</i> goes before the verb: <i>no corras</i>, <i>no está</i>. In English you need <b>don't</b> or <b>isn't</b>: <b>Don't run.</b> <b>He isn't here.</b>",
      "<b>No</b> alone is only the answer: <i>Can I use the lift? — No.</i>"
     ],
     "avoid": "No run. · He no is here. · No use the lift."
    },
    {
     "type": "choice",
     "q": "Which one is correct?",
     "opts": [
      "He isn't here.",
      "He no is here.",
      "He not here."
     ],
     "a": 0
    },
    {
     "type": "tip",
     "tag": "Pronunciation",
     "title": "Catch the NOT",
     "paras": [
      "In fast English, <i>isn't</i> and <i>don't</i> are short. Listen for the <b>t</b> at the end, or for <b>not</b>, stressed.",
      "In an announcement the speaker says <b>NOT</b> slowly, so nobody misses it."
     ],
